# Record 123: the rank-one model.
talk(123,'1967-03-06','A. A. Kirillov','P000198','The Lorentzian rank-one model for invariant regularity','talk',r'''A short final Kirillov note isolates the form \(x^2+y^2-z^2\), its wave operator, and invariant distributions annihilated by a polynomial in that operator. The question of local summability is the rank-one Lie-algebra case of the theorem developed in the preceding lectures. Arnold's report begins later on the same page.''')
rid=item(123,'P000198','question','Local integrability of Lorentz-invariant polynomial wave solutions',r'''For \(q=x^2+y^2-z^2\), \(T\) is invariant under transformations preserving \(q\), and \(P(\partial_x^2+\partial_y^2-\partial_z^2)T=0\). The notes ask: is \(T\) locally summable?''',r'''Let \(T\in\mathcal D'(\mathbb R^3)\) be invariant under \(SO_0(2,1)\), and let \(0\ne P\in\mathbb C[t]\) satisfy \(P(\Box)T=0\), where \(\Box=\partial_x^2+\partial_y^2-\partial_z^2\). Then \(T\in L^1_{\mathrm{loc}}(\mathbb R^3)\). Full \(O(2,1)\)-invariance is more than needed.''',r'''A linear identification with \(\mathfrak{sl}_2(\mathbb R)\) identifies \(q\), up to a nonzero scalar, with its invariant quadratic form. The adjoint group is \(SO_0(2,1)\), and the invariant constant-coefficient algebra is generated by \(\Box\); \((P)\) has finite codimension. The nonzero-polynomial hypothesis is essential.''',fate=r'''Already covered by \citet[Theorem~1; \S2]{harishchandra1965algebra}; the notebook question is an expository specialization.''',status=r'''As of 9 November 2026, the answer is affirmative. It is not a surviving open regularity problem.''',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='exact',refs=['harishchandra1965algebra'],problem='GP-1966-60')
P.append(dict(id='GP-1966-60',title='Invariant distributions for the three-dimensional Lorentz form',statement=r'''For \(SO_0(2,1)\)-invariant \(T\in\mathcal D'(\mathbb R^3)\) and nonzero \(P\in\mathbb C[t]\), does \(P(\partial_x^2+\partial_y^2-\partial_z^2)T=0\) force \(T\in L^1_{\mathrm{loc}}\)?''',occurrences=[rid],status=r'''Solved before this occurrence: the affirmative answer is the rank-one case of \citet[Theorem~1]{harishchandra1965algebra}. Checked as of 9 November 2026. No identical precise question was located in the supplied earlier index.''',status_date='2026-11',refs=['harishchandra1965algebra']))
item(123,'P000198','method','Why the invariant system is elliptic away from the null cone',r'''The notes say that away from \(q=0\) the system is elliptic, referring to invariant equations plus the polynomial in the wave operator.''',r'''On each regular orbit component with \(q\ne0\), an invariant local function or distribution depends on the transverse variable \(s=q\). The radial expression is \(\Box f(q)=4q f''(q)+6f'(q)\). Thus a nonzero polynomial in \(\Box\) yields an ordinary differential equation whose leading coefficient is nonzero for \(q\ne0\). This proves analytic regularity there; the null cone, including its vertex, requires the invariant-distribution theorem.''',r'''This is ellipticity after orbit reduction, or of the full invariant system; the scalar wave operator is not elliptic on \(\mathbb R^3\). \oursComputed\ The radial formula is an elementary reconstruction checked by applying the chain rule separately to all three coordinates.''',fate=r'''The general regular-set argument appears in \citet[\S2, Lemma~1]{harishchandra1965algebra}.''',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',refs=['harishchandra1965algebra'],development='annotated',match='partial')
save()
# Record 124 and the uncatalogued Anosov subsection adjacent to it.
talk(124,'1967-03-06','V. I. Arnold','P000198-P000199','Surface diffeomorphism groups; adjacent Anosov note on operator Fourier transformation','talk',r'''Arnold reports Earle--Eells on the topology of the identity component of the diffeomorphism group of an oriented surface, with genus-by-genus answers and the quasiconformal proof strategy. P000199 begins a distinct, explicit \ru{Аносов} heading and develops an operator-valued Fourier transform attributed to Maslov. The catalogue supplies no separate record for this subsection; its three items are retained here with Anosov as the item-level speaker. This grouping preserves source coverage without attributing Anosov's mathematics to Arnold.''')
item(124,'P000198','theorem','Diffeomorphisms of the sphere fixing three points',r'''For \(X=S^2\), the notes give \(\operatorname{Diff}_0X=G\times\operatorname{Diff}(S^2-a,b,c)\), where \(G\) is the conformal group and the second factor is contractible; the three points are to stay fixed.''',r'''Give smooth diffeomorphism groups the \(C^\infty\) topology. For three ordered distinct points \(a,b,c\in S^2\), the subgroup of orientation-preserving diffeomorphisms fixing them pointwise is contractible, and composition gives a homeomorphism \[\operatorname{Diff}_0(S^2)\simeq PSL_2(\mathbb C)\times\operatorname{Diff}_0(S^2;a,b,c).\] Consequently \(\operatorname{Diff}_0(S^2)\) has the homotopy type of \(SO(3)\).''',r'''The puncture notation means fixed marked points, not arbitrary diffeomorphisms of an open punctured surface. The product is a homeomorphism of spaces, not generally a direct-product decomposition of groups.''',fate=r'''\citet[Theorem~1(a)]{earleeells1967diffeomorphism}, communicated 6 February 1967, printed July 1967. The notebook explicitly reports Earle--Eells; separate public circulation before March was not established.''',refs=['earleeells1967diffeomorphism'],match='exact',literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(124,'P000198','theorem','Diffeomorphisms of the torus fixing one point',r'''For \(X=T^2\), the notes give \(\operatorname{Diff}_0(T^2)=T^2\times\operatorname{Diff}(T^2-a)\), with the fixed-point factor contractible.''',r'''The evaluation at a point and the translation action identify \(\operatorname{Diff}_0(T^2)\) homeomorphically with \(T^2\times\operatorname{Diff}_0(T^2;a)\). The subgroup fixing \(a\) and homotopic to the identity is contractible. Thus \(\operatorname{Diff}_0(T^2)\simeq T^2\).''',r'''Homotopy to the identity is part of the fixed-point subgroup; arbitrary mapping classes fixing the point are not included.''',fate=r'''\citet[Theorem~1(b)]{earleeells1967diffeomorphism}; the manuscript was communicated in February and printed in July 1967; the precise first public circulation is not established.''',refs=['earleeells1967diffeomorphism'],match='exact',literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(124,'P000198','theorem','Contractibility in genus at least two',r'''For genus at least \(2\), \(\operatorname{Diff}_0X\) is declared contractible.''',r'''If \(X\) is a closed, connected, oriented smooth surface of genus \(g\ge2\), its smooth diffeomorphisms homotopic to the identity form the identity component, and this topological group is contractible in the \(C^\infty\) topology.''',r'''Closedness, connectedness, and the topology on the group are supplied; boundary and puncture variants are separate statements.''',fate=r'''\citet[Theorem~1(c), Corollary]{earleeells1967diffeomorphism}; communicated to the journal in February 1967 and expressly reported as their result in these March notes.''',refs=['earleeells1967diffeomorphism'],match='exact',literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(124,'P000198','method','Quasiconformal uniformization and the Teichmüller fibration',r'''The proof is said to use Ahlfors and Bers on quasiconformal maps, ``onto which any diffeomorphism contracts.''''',r'''The rigorous strategy uses the contractible space of smooth conformal structures, the contractible Teichm\"uller space, and a principal action of the appropriate identity or marked-point diffeomorphism group. The resulting fibration and the manifold properties of the group imply contractibility of the relevant fiber. Quasiconformal Beltrami equations provide the required parametrization and local sections.''',r'''The notes do not describe a literal deformation of every diffeomorphism ``onto quasiconformal maps''; smooth surface diffeomorphisms already are quasiconformal for suitable bounds. The fibration, rather than that phrase, is the substantive argument.''',fate=r'''The exact proof outline is \citet[Theorem~2; \S\S3--4]{earleeells1967diffeomorphism}.''',refs=['earleeells1967diffeomorphism'],match='exact',literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(124,'P000198','remark','The diffeomorphism group as an infinite-dimensional Lie group',r'''The final sentence calls \(\operatorname{Diff}\) a Lie group in a new sense of smooth structure.''',r'''For a compact smooth manifold, \(\operatorname{Diff}(X)\) admits an infinite-dimensional smooth Lie-group structure modeled locally on the Fr\'echet space of smooth vector fields. This requires a suitable infinite-dimensional differentiability calculus; it is not a finite-dimensional Lie group.''',r'''The notebook specifies no calculus or regularity theorem. The contemporary reference is Leslie, as cited by Earle--Eells; Leslie's full article was not accessible in this run.''',fate=r'''\citet[Remark~2 and reference~8]{earleeells1967diffeomorphism} cites Leslie's then-forthcoming 1967 construction.''',refs=['earleeells1967diffeomorphism'],verification='indirect',match='partial',literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(124,'P000199','construction','Semiclassical Fourier transformation of the Schrödinger operator',r'''Under the Anosov heading, \(-\tfrac{h^2}{2}\partial_x^2+U(x)\) transforms to \(\tfrac{p^2}{2}+U(\tfrac h i\partial_p)\); the Fourier kernel is \(e^{\pm i\langle x,p\rangle/h}\).''',r'''For \(h>0\), use the unitary transform \((\mathcal F_h^+f)(p)=(2\pi h)^{-1/2}\int e^{ixp/h}f(x)\,dx\). On Schwartz functions and for polynomial \(U\), \[\mathcal F_h^+\left(-\frac{h^2}{2}\partial_x^2+U(x)\right)(\mathcal F_h^+)^{-1}=\frac{p^2}{2}+U(-ih\partial_p).\] For a more general potential the notation on the right denotes the corresponding Fourier multiplier/convolution or functional-calculus operator on its appropriate domain.''',r'''The positive Fourier sign matches the notebook's \(h/i\). The polynomial/core case supplies a precise statement without assuming an arbitrary potential is a differential operator.''',fate=r'''An elementary preexisting Fourier identity, used in Maslov's 1965 programme; only the book's metadata were checked \citep{maslov1965perturbation}.''',refs=['maslov1965perturbation'],verification='publisher metadata checked',match='thematic only',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',speaker='D. V. Anosov')
item(124,'P000199','construction','A Fourier transform with a self-adjoint operator as reciprocal Planck constant',r'''For Hilbert space \(H\), self-adjoint \(A\) with \(0\) not an eigenvalue, the notes replace \(1/h\) by \(A\) and insert the normalization \(|A|^{n/2}/(\sqrt{2\pi})^n\) in \(\phi^A f(p)=\int e^{iA\langle x,p\rangle}f(x)\,dx\).''',r'''A precise interpretation uses the spectral decomposition \(A=\int\lambda\,dE(\lambda)\), with \(E(\{0\})=0\). On each spectral fiber define \[\mathcal F_\lambda^+f(p)=\left(\frac{|\lambda|}{2\pi}\right)^{n/2}\int_{\mathbb R^n}e^{i\lambda\langle x,p\rangle}f(x)\,dx.\] The direct integral of these unitary transforms defines a unitary \(\mathcal F_A^+\) on \(L^2(\mathbb R^n;H)\). The notebook's integral formula is literal on a suitable spectral/Schwartz core and otherwise is understood through this extension.''',r'''\ours\ The direct-integral interpretation is supplied here. It allows \(0\) in continuous spectrum: ``not an eigenvalue'' does not imply \(A^{-1}\) is bounded. Fiberwise Plancherel verifies the normalization; substitution \(q=\lambda p\) independently gives the same norm identity. No exact occurrence of this operator-valued formula was located in the checked Maslov metadata.''',fate=r'''Maslov's 1965 book is the stated historical context, but the precise transform-to-publication match is unresolved \citep{maslov1965perturbation}.''',status=r'''As of 9 November 2026, the spectral construction above is well defined. The attribution is only thematic; no new general quantization theorem is inferred.''',refs=['maslov1965perturbation'],verification='indirect',match='thematic only',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',development='annotated',confidence='medium',speaker='D. V. Anosov')
item(124,'P000199','method','Formal operator-valued quantization of L(x,p,A inverse)',r'''The note ends with \(\mathcal L(x,\widehat p,A^{-1})f=\overline{\phi^A}_{p\to x}L(x,p,A^{-1})\phi^A_{x\to p}f\), after posing \(Lu=f\) with \(u(x)\in H\). No symbol estimates, domain, ordering convention, or solvability theorem is supplied.''',r'''As a formal quantization rule the expression indicates applying an \(A\)-scaled Fourier transform, a symbol, and inverse transformation with \(x\) retained as an external parameter. It does not define a unique operator for arbitrary noncommuting operator-valued \(L\). A rigorous restricted construction is possible when \(L\) is decomposable with respect to \(A\), its scalar spectral fibers satisfy stated pseudodifferential symbol bounds, and common domains and an ordering convention are fixed.''',r'''The decomposability and symbol assumptions describe one possible implementation, not a recovered claim by Anosov. The unresolved issue here is the missing statement, not a demonstrated open research problem.''',cls='F',fate=r'''The Maslov book provides a thematic lead only; neither its catalogue nor the notebook identifies this general theorem \citep{maslov1965perturbation}.''',status=r'''As of 9 November 2026, the unrestricted construction and the intended solvability result remain unidentified from these notes.''',refs=['maslov1965perturbation'],verification='unresolved lead',match='thematic only',literal='not determinable',confidence='low',speaker='D. V. Anosov')
correction('P000198-P000199','speaker ownership','Catalogue record 124 is Arnold; P000199 begins Anosov','Keep the catalogue record, but attribute P000199 mathematical items to D. V. Anosov.','The scan has a separate explicit Anosov heading. No additional record is present in the supplied 38-record catalogue.','both','provenance')
correction('P000198','sphere and torus stabilizer notation','Diff(S^2-a,b,c), Diff(T^2-a)','Diffeomorphisms in the specified identity class fixing the marked points, not unrestricted diffeomorphisms of punctured surfaces.','The scan says the points stay in place. Earle–Eells Theorem 1 uses pointwise marked-point stabilizers.','both','ambiguity')
correction('P000199','operator inverse and Fourier normalization','0 is not an eigenvalue; use A inverse','A inverse may be unbounded; define the transform spectrally with the inserted factor |A|^(n/2)/(2 pi)^(n/2).','The scan confirms both the weak spectral hypothesis and the separately inserted normalization. The implication to a bounded inverse would be invalid.','both','ambiguity')
save()

# Record 125: Parshin on Mumford.
talk(125,'1967-03-13','A. N. Parshin','P000200','Algebraic group actions and geometric orbit quotients','talk',r'''Parshin's report on Mumford defines a regular algebraic action and asks when its orbit set carries an algebraic quotient structure. It records stabilizer semicontinuity, necessary restrictions on a morphism whose fibers are individual orbits, and the affine reductive closed-orbit criterion in compressed form. An unlabelled hyperbola-like sketch cannot be uniquely identified. The later projective continuation, record 128, preserves only a heading.''')
item(125,'P000200','definition','Regular algebraic actions and orbits',r'''A regular map \(\sigma:G\times X\to X\) is illustrated by an associativity square, whose top arrow is labelled \(\sigma\times1\); equivalent points form an orbit.''',r'''Over an algebraically closed field \(k\), an algebraic action of an algebraic group \(G\) on a variety \(X\) is a morphism \(\sigma:G\times X\to X\) satisfying \(\sigma(e,x)=x\) and \(\sigma(g,\sigma(h,x))=\sigma(gh,x)\). In the standard ordered product \(G\times G\times X\), the top map of the associativity square is \(1_G\times\sigma\).''',r'''The identity axiom is supplied. The stated top-arrow label has the wrong factor order unless a permutation of factors is additionally declared.''',fate=r'''Standard setup in the 1965 book \citep{mumford1965invariant}; modern definitions are in \citet[\S2.1]{berczi2016quotients}.''',refs=['mumford1965invariant','berczi2016quotients'],match='exact',literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
rid=item(125,'P000200','question','When does an orbit set admit an algebraic quotient?',r'''The explicit question is when \(X/G\), meaning the set of orbits, is an algebraic variety and in what sense the map \(X\to X/G\) is regular.''',r'''For an affine variety \(X\) over an algebraically closed field of characteristic zero and a reductive algebraic group \(G\), the invariant ring is finitely generated and \(q:X\to\operatorname{Spec}k[X]^G\) is a categorical quotient. Its fibers identify orbit-closure equivalence classes and contain one closed orbit; it is a geometric orbit quotient of all \(X\) precisely when all orbits are closed. For an arbitrary algebraic action, a geometric quotient exists on a suitable nonempty invariant open subset; it need not exist on all of \(X\).''',r'''Affine reductive hypotheses make the notes' final criterion precise. The distinction between a categorical quotient and a quotient whose fibers are individual orbits is essential. These are answers to definite versions of the stated question, not an assertion of a single universal global quotient theorem for all varieties.''',fate=r'''Known before the report from Mumford/Rosenlicht; compare \citet[Theorems~2.1.10, 2.2.1]{berczi2016quotients}.''',status=r'''As of 9 November 2026, the stated affine reductive question and the generic-open existence theorem are established. The unrestricted wording needs a chosen quotient category.''',refs=['mumford1965invariant','berczi2016quotients'],match='partial',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',problem='GP-1966-61')
P.append(dict(id='GP-1966-61',title='Geometric orbit quotients for affine reductive actions',statement=r'''For an affine variety over an algebraically closed field of characteristic zero with a reductive group action, when does the invariant-theoretic categorical quotient have individual orbits as its fibers?''',occurrences=[rid],status=r'''Solved before the March 1967 report: exactly when all orbits are closed. The modern verification is \citet[Theorem~2.2.1 and Definition~2.1.4]{berczi2016quotients}, in the framework of \citet{mumford1965invariant}. The broader notebook question is recorded with its quotient-category ambiguity. Status checked 9 November 2026.''',status_date='2026-11',refs=['mumford1965invariant','berczi2016quotients']))
item(125,'P000200','remark','Unlabelled orbit-space counterexample sketch',r'''A drawing of axes and curved branches accompanies the statement that no algebraic structure can be introduced until a point is removed. Neither the group, action, variety, nor removed point is labelled.''',r'''There is insufficient information to state a unique example or a counterexample. The picture is consistent with several torus actions having nonclosed orbits, but neither the orbit partition nor the intended separation condition can be recovered.''',r'''The familiar action \(t\cdot(x,y)=(tx,t^{-1}y)\) is not installed as the missing example: even there, deleting the origin has subtleties involving nonseparated orbit spaces.''',cls='F',fate=r'''The general quotient literature \citep[\S2.1]{berczi2016quotients} does not identify an unlabelled diagram.''',status=r'''As of 9 November 2026, this particular example remains unidentified; it does not establish an orphan open problem.''',refs=['berczi2016quotients'],match='none found',verification='unresolved lead',literal='not determinable',confidence='low')
item(125,'P000200','theorem','Semicontinuity of stabilizer and orbit dimensions',r'''The stationary subgroup may have larger dimension than the generic stationary subgroup.''',r'''For an algebraic group acting on a variety, the function \(x\mapsto\dim G_x\) is upper semicontinuous, and \(\dim(Gx)=\dim G-\dim G_x\) is lower semicontinuous. On an irreducible variety the generic stabilizer dimension is minimal and the generic orbit dimension maximal.''',r'''Dimension is meant scheme-theoretically or geometrically consistently; the characteristic-zero setting in the notes avoids nonreduced stabilizer complications.''',fate=r'''Classical action geometry; see \citet[\S2.2, discussion preceding Theorem~2.2.1]{berczi2016quotients}.''',refs=['berczi2016quotients'],match='exact',literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(125,'P000200','theorem','Necessary conditions on a regular morphism with orbit fibers',r'''If \(X/G\) has an algebraic structure and \(X\to X/G\) is regular, all orbits must have the same dimension and, in particular, be closed.''',r'''Let \(X,Y\) be irreducible varieties over an algebraically closed field, and let \(q:X\to Y\) be a surjective morphism whose set-theoretic fibers over closed points are exactly the \(G\)-orbits. Then all such orbits are closed and have dimension \(\dim X-\dim Y\). Indeed fiber dimension is at least this number, while orbit dimension is at most its generic value, which equals it.''',r'''\ours\ Irreducibility, surjectivity, and the single-orbit fiber condition make the notebook claim valid. Closedness follows directly from being a fiber, not merely from equal orbit dimensions. Independently, the generic fiber-dimension theorem and stabilizer semicontinuity give the two opposite inequalities, checking the argument.''',fate=r'''A standard consequence of algebraic orbit and fiber dimensions in the quotient framework \citep[\S2.1]{berczi2016quotients}.''',refs=['berczi2016quotients'],match='partial',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',development='annotated')
item(125,'P000200','theorem','Closed orbits and the affine reductive geometric quotient',r'''For a reductive group in characteristic zero, the notes end with ``a quotient exists if and only if all orbits are closed.''''',r'''If \(G\) is reductive, \(\operatorname{char}k=0\), and \(X\) is affine, the invariant quotient \(q:X\to X/\!/G\) exists whether or not every orbit is closed. It is geometric, with precisely one orbit in each fiber, if and only if every orbit is closed. Closedness alone is not the stated existence criterion for an arbitrary nonaffine variety or for an unspecified quotient category.''',r'''The missing words are ``affine'' and ``geometric.'' The literal statement is false when ``quotient'' means categorical quotient. This distinguishes a valid old theorem in shorthand from a new existence problem.''',fate=r'''The precise affine theorem is \citet[Theorem~2.2.1]{berczi2016quotients}, reflecting the prelecture Mumford theory \citep{mumford1965invariant}.''',refs=['mumford1965invariant','berczi2016quotients'],match='exact',literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
correction('P000200','associativity square top arrow','sigma times 1','1_G times sigma for the displayed order G times G times X.','The scan clearly has sigma times 1. The domain of the action is G times X, so the labelled top map is ill typed in the displayed factor order.','both','faithful_error')
correction('P000200','quotient criterion','quotient exists iff all orbits closed','For an affine reductive action, the invariant quotient is geometric iff all orbits are closed; the categorical quotient already exists.','The scan does not state affine or specify geometric. These hypotheses are required by the standard GIT theorem.','both','ambiguity')
save()

# Record 126: axioms for index.
talk(126,'1967-03-13','A. S. Dynin','P000201','Stable symbols and an axiomatic index','talk',r'''Dynin's second lecture defines compactly supported stable symbols and lists homotopy invariance, additivity, products, excision, vanishing for symbols from the base, and the one-dimensional winding normalization. The first isolated uniqueness claim mentions only the first two properties and is false literally; the later full family of axioms supplies the intended setting. This is related to the earlier index lectures, but no already-indexed open question is reasserted.''')
item(126,'P000201','definition','Compactly supported stable symbol classes',r'''\(\Sigma(\mathbb R^n)\) consists of maps \(\mathbb R^n\times S^{n-1}\to GL_\infty(\mathbb C)\), equal to \(1\) outside \(K\times S^{n-1}\). The general version uses \(T_0(X)\) and a compact support in the base.''',r'''A stable invertible principal symbol on \(X\), trivial outside a compact subset of the base, is represented by a map \(S^*X\to GL_N(\mathbb C)\), stabilized by identity blocks and considered through homotopy and the relation of extending from the base. Clutching over the cotangent disk bundle yields its compactly supported \(K\)-theory symbol class in \(K_c^0(T^*X)\). A metric identifies tangent and cotangent sphere bundles when necessary.''',r'''The raw maps are not themselves already all of \(K_c^0(T^*X)\); the relations and, for general bundles, bundle-valued symbols are needed. \(T_0\) is interpreted as the punctured cotangent bundle with degree-zero homogeneity, or equivalently its cosphere.''',fate=r'''Stable symbol and \(K\)-theory framework: \citet[\S2; \S3.1]{dynin1966index}.''',refs=['dynin1966index'],match='partial',literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(126,'P000201','claim','Homotopy invariance and direct-sum additivity do not determine the index',r'''Above a dividing line the notes claim a unique \(\operatorname{ind}:\Sigma(\mathbb R^n)\to\mathbb Z\) with only (I) homotopy invariance and (II) direct-sum additivity.''',r'''These two axioms alone cannot normalize a nonzero index: the zero map and every integer multiple of any additive homotopy invariant satisfy them. Already for \(n=1\), \[w(\sigma)=\operatorname{wind}\det\sigma(\cdot,+1)-\operatorname{wind}\det\sigma(\cdot,-1)\] is a nonzero such invariant for compactly supported stable symbols; \(0,w,2w\) are distinct.''',r'''\ours\ The counterexample uses a smooth phase that rises from \(0\) to \(2\pi\) in a compact interval on the positive component and is constant on the negative component. Determinant multiplication under direct sum proves additivity; homotopy invariance follows from integer winding. The later normalization axiom fixes this defect.''',fate=r'''Dynin's actual index properties include a normalization: \citet[\S3.1; \S3.2.10]{dynin1966index}.''',status=r'''As of 9 November 2026, the isolated uniqueness statement is false; the normalized index theory remains established.''',refs=['dynin1966index'],match='partial',literal='false as written',development='annotated')
item(126,'P000201','theorem','Multiplicativity under external products',r'''A product \(\#:\Sigma(X)\times\Sigma(Y)\to\Sigma(X\times Y)\) satisfies \(\operatorname{ind}(\sigma_X\#\sigma_Y)=\operatorname{ind}\sigma_X\operatorname{ind}\sigma_Y\).''',r'''For compactly supported elliptic \(K\)-theory symbols, their external product corresponds to the graded tensor product of elliptic complexes. The analytic index is multiplicative on this product, as is the topological index. The symbol product is not merely pointwise multiplication of two unrelated square matrices on different cosphere bundles.''',r'''The external-product construction is supplied; the notes state only its name and index identity. Stable triviality of tangent bundles is part of the lecture's restricted family, not a necessary hypothesis of the modern product theorem.''',fate=r'''\citet[\S3.1.6]{dynin1966index} gives the prelecture product property.''',refs=['dynin1966index'],match='exact',literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(126,'P000201','theorem','Excision for compactly supported index classes',r'''If \(\operatorname{supp}\sigma\subset U\subset X\), the index on \(U\) equals the index on \(X\).''',r'''For an open embedding \(j:U\hookrightarrow X\) and a compactly supported elliptic symbol class \(a\in K_c^0(T^*U)\), extension by the identity/zero symbol class defines \(j_!a\), and \(\operatorname{ind}_X(j_!a)=\operatorname{ind}_U(a)\). The relevant support is the nontrivial part of the symbol class, not the support of an everywhere invertible matrix as a function.''',r'''This is an index statement for symbols trivial at infinity; an arbitrary differential operator on a noncompact open manifold need not be Fredholm without this interpretation.''',fate=r'''A standard topological-index property in the framework of \citet[\S\S2--3]{dynin1966index}; exact six-axiom wording was not found there.''',refs=['dynin1966index'],match='partial',literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(126,'P000201','theorem','Symbols pulled back from the base have zero index',r'''A commuting diagram \(T_0(X)\to X\to GL\) implies \(\operatorname{ind}\sigma=0\).''',r'''An invertible symbol that extends over the cotangent disk bundle, in particular one pulled back from a bundle automorphism on \(X\), has zero relative \(K\)-theory class and zero index. Analytically, multiplication by the corresponding invertible bundle map is an invertible order-zero realization.''',r'''The compact-support/trivial-at-infinity convention from the definition is retained. This explains why raw homotopy classes on the cosphere require a quotient by extendible symbols.''',fate=r'''An elementary consequence of the symbol construction in \citet[\S2]{dynin1966index}.''',refs=['dynin1966index'],match='partial')
item(126,'P000201','theorem','One-dimensional winding normalization',r'''For \(X=\mathbb R\), the Noether--Muskhelishvili formula is \(\operatorname{ind}\sigma=(2\pi)^{-1}\Delta_{\mathbb R\times S^0}\arg\det\sigma\).''',r'''For a smooth stable invertible symbol equal to the identity off a compact subset of \(\mathbb R\), each of its two cosphere components closes to a loop. With the boundary orientation and Fourier/index sign convention chosen consistently, the index is the oriented difference of their determinant winding numbers. Reversing the Fourier or boundary orientation convention reverses the displayed sign.''',r'''The unoriented notation \(\mathbb R\times S^0\) is insufficient to determine the sign; no independent sign is silently imposed on the notebook.''',fate=r'''The normalized dimension-one calculation is \citet[\S3.2.10]{dynin1966index}.''',refs=['dynin1966index'],match='exact',literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(126,'P000201','claim','A normalized index characterization on stably parallelizable manifolds',r'''Below the line, the notes assert existence and uniqueness of an index for manifolds with stably trivial tangent bundle satisfying (I)--(VI).''',r'''The intended object is the normalized index homomorphism on compactly supported stable elliptic symbol classes, natural under excision and external products and normalized on the one-dimensional Bott generator. In a precise \(K\)-theoretic axiomatization, Bott periodicity and compatible pushforward determine the usual topological index, equal to the analytic index. The six shorthand lines require a specified symbol category, product construction, and stabilization before a literal uniqueness theorem can be tested.''',r'''The modern normalized index statement is supplied as an interpretation. No exact proof of the notebook's six-axiom formulation, restricted to stably parallelizable manifolds, was located in the opened Dynin exposition; it is therefore not reported as a verbatim published theorem.''',fate=r'''The 1966 exposition proves the index formula and required properties \citep[\S3.2.1]{dynin1966index}; the exact axiom list matches only partially.''',status=r'''As of 9 November 2026, the normalized analytic/topological index is established. The bare six-line formulation is under-specified, not evidence of an open uniqueness problem.''',refs=['dynin1966index'],match='partial',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',confidence='medium')
correction('P000201','first uniqueness theorem','Unique index follows from homotopy invariance and direct-sum additivity alone','Normalization and additional functorial axioms are required; zero and integer multiples are counterexamples to uniqueness.','The scan isolates only I and II above the dividing line. The lower block adds III–VI, including winding normalization.','both','faithful_error')
correction('P000201','one-dimensional winding formula','Delta arg on R times S^0 without orientation','Specify opposite component orientations and the Fourier/index sign convention.','Both components have a winding number; a numerical signed index requires their oriented difference.','both','ambiguity')
save()

# Record 127: the March 27 boundary-family lecture.
talk(127,'1967-03-27','A. S. Dynin','P000202-P000204','Boundary systems with varying partial indices and global completion','talk',r'''The March 27 material begins below the separate March 20 Parshin heading on P000202. The catalogue attributes this record to Dynin, but the manuscript names Eskin on P000203; this could introduce Eskin's work or a further speaker, and the catalogue attribution is retained with that qualification. The lecture treats a continuous family of normal half-line problems even when individual partial indices jump, auxiliary potentials and boundary equations, localization, and a global completion question. The latter matches the framework and criterion of Vishik--Eskin's paper received 27 April 1967, rather than merely a later paper on the same subject.''')
item(127,'P000202-P000203','construction','Normal matrix factorization and partial indices',r'''The factorization is \(A(\omega,\eta_n)=A_-M_-M_+A_+\), with \(M_+=\operatorname{diag}((\eta_n+i)^{n_j(\omega)})\). The integers \(n_j\) may depend on \(\omega\). In the full variables \(A_\pm\) have degree zero and the order factor \(\xi_-^\alpha\) is included in \(M_-\).''',r'''For an elliptic matrix symbol, homogeneous of order \(\alpha+i\beta\) and with the regularity and normal-limit assumptions of the convolution calculus, fix a nonzero tangential direction \(\omega\). Remove the scalar order and normal-jump factors; the normalized matrix admits Wiener--Hopf factorization with integer partial indices \(n_j(\omega)\). The unreduced partial indices are \(\kappa_j=n_j+\gamma_j\), where \(\gamma_j\) arise from a chosen logarithm of the normal jump. Such pointwise factorizations need not vary continuously when the integer indices change.''',r'''The normalization, half-plane analyticity of the factors, and distinction between integer and full partial indices are supplied. No continuously chosen factorization is inferred from pointwise factorization.''',fate=r'''The pointwise factorization was already available: \citet[\S4]{vishikeskin1967elliptic}, received December 1965; the later notation is exact in \citet[\S1.3]{vishikeskin1967normally}.''',refs=['vishikeskin1967elliptic','vishikeskin1967normally'],match='exact',literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(127,'P000202','theorem','Fredholm normal families despite jumping kernel dimensions',r'''The notes emphasize \(n_j(\omega)\) need not be constant and nevertheless assert a regularizer construction; the dimensions are denoted \(m_+(\omega)\) and \(m_-(\omega)\).''',r'''Let \(A_\omega\) be the half-line convolution family obtained by freezing the boundary point and unit tangential covector. Choose Sobolev orders avoiding the critical values \(\operatorname{Re}\kappa_j+\tfrac12+\mathbb Z\), continuously over the parameter set, and identify the resulting spaces with fixed Hilbert spaces. Under the stated symbol regularity, \(A_\omega\) is a norm-continuous Fredholm family. Its kernel and cokernel dimensions \(m_+\) and \(m_-\) can jump, but their difference is constant on each connected component and both dimensions are bounded on a compact parameter space.''',r'''Uniform admissibility of the Sobolev order is essential; arbitrary elliptic symbols and arbitrary orders are not covered. Continuity concerns the Fredholm operators, not a chosen kernel basis or factorization.''',cls='B',fate=r'''The corresponding result is \citet[\S1, Lemmas~1.1--3.1 and Remark~1.1]{vishikeskin1967normally}, received 27 April 1967 and published that year.''',status=r'''As of 9 November 2026, the qualified family theorem is established. Its verified 1967 proof expressly removes the earlier constant-integer-index restriction.''',refs=['vishikeskin1967normally'],match='exact',literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(127,'P000202','construction','Auxiliary potentials and boundary equations',r'''The one-dimensional system is \(Au_++G\rho=\ell f+u_-\), \((B,u_+)_{\eta_n}+E\rho=g\), with \(\rho\in\mathbb C^M\), \(\ell-M=m_+-m_-\), unique solvability, and a continuously dependent inverse \(R_\omega\). The same glyph \(\ell\) is used for extension of \(f\) and for the number of boundary equations.''',r'''For a continuous Fredholm family \(A_\omega:H_1\to H_2\), seek a block completion \[\mathcal A_\omega=\begin{pmatrix}A_\omega&G_\omega\\B_\omega&E_\omega\end{pmatrix}:H_1\oplus\mathbb C^M\longrightarrow H_2\oplus\mathbb C^\ell\] that is invertible. A necessary index balance is \(\ell-M=\operatorname{ind}A_\omega\). Such completions exist locally in the parameter; once the block is norm-continuous and invertible, \(R_\omega=\mathcal A_\omega^{-1}\) is norm-continuous. In the convolution problem, \(G\) is a potential matrix and \(B\) a boundary functional with prescribed homogeneity and Sobolev mapping properties. The index balance alone does not ensure a single global completion.''',r'''The undefined boundary-restriction subscript on \(g\) is not used to recover a new space. Abstract local completion and continuity of inversion explain the claim; global existence is separated as the later explicit question.''',cls='B',fate=r'''The same augmented system and algebraic solvability condition appear in \citet[\S1.5, equations (44.1)--(45.1)]{vishikeskin1967normally}.''',status=r'''As of 9 November 2026, this completion construction and its parameter continuity are standard under the stated assumptions; the global obstruction is recorded separately.''',refs=['vishikeskin1967normally'],match='exact',literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(127,'P000203','method','Homogeneous lifting and localization of normal inverses',r'''The normal construction is transferred to \(n\) dimensions by factoring out \(|\xi'|\); left and right regularizers are obtained by localization. The margin adds \ru{Якобы} beside the reference to Simonenko's abstract treatment of inverse operators depending on parameters.''',r'''Suppose an elliptic convolution system on a smooth bounded domain has an admissible continuous normal family and globally specified potential/boundary symbols of the required orders, whose augmented normal problem is invertible at every boundary covector. Rescaling the normal inverse by tangential homogeneity and patching local parametrices yields left and right regularizers modulo compact operators on the designated Sobolev spaces. Hence the global boundary system is Fredholm. A formal collection of pointwise inverses without symbol estimates, admissible orders, and compatible global completion is insufficient.''',r'''The hypotheses are those of the matching theorem, not an assertion that arbitrary parameter dependence can be localized. The marginal reservation is retained as evidence, not interpreted as a refutation of Simonenko.''',cls='B',fate=r'''The precise half-space and bounded-domain results are in \citet[pp.~316, 322--325]{vishikeskin1967normally}. Simonenko's 1965 paper is a metadata-verified lead only \citep{simonenko1965method}.''',status=r'''As of 9 November 2026, the qualified Fredholm construction is established. The exact Simonenko passage meant by the speaker remains unverified.''',refs=['vishikeskin1967normally','simonenko1965method'],match='exact',literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(127,'P000203','definition','Normal jump logarithms and admissible Sobolev orders',r'''Under the heading \ru{Эскин}, the notes set \(a_\pm(x')=A(x',0,0,\pm1)\), \(b=a_+^{-1}a_-\), and \(\gamma=(2\pi i)^{-1}\log b\). They demand globally continuous eigenvalue branches with \(|\operatorname{Re}\gamma_i-\operatorname{Re}\gamma_j|<1\), calling this \(\widehat{\mathcal E}_\alpha\). The jump is called a measure of nonellipticity.''',r'''For a zero-order normalized elliptic symbol at a smooth boundary, the two invertible normal limits define a normal jump \(b=a_+^{-1}a_-\). Locally one can choose a matrix logarithm and spectral branches in a strip of width less than one. Global branch choices require compatibility. For nonzero symbol order, first normalize the order; in Vishik--Eskin's convention the negative limit includes the phase \(e^{i\pi(\alpha+i\beta)}\). A continuous Sobolev-order function avoiding all critical fractional indices is the functional-analytic admissibility condition.''',r'''The jump measures normal factorization data, not a failure of interior ellipticity. The notebook's symbol for the class is uncertain, and its global enumeration of eigenvalue branches is stronger than a merely pointwise logarithm. The later paper uses a precisely defined class and a noncritical order condition rather than relying on this compressed name.''',cls='B',fate=r'''The normalized limits and admissibility condition are explicit in \citet[\S2, equations (1.2)--(6.2)]{vishikeskin1967normally}; local logarithmic factorization predates the lecture \citep[\S4]{vishikeskin1967elliptic}.''',status=r'''As of 9 November 2026, the normalized construction is established; the notebook's class label has only a partial exact identification.''',refs=['vishikeskin1967normally','vishikeskin1967elliptic'],match='partial',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',confidence='medium')
item(127,'P000203-P000204','remark','Diagonal systems and the limitation of a common ordinary space',r'''For diagonal \(A=\operatorname{diag}(a_j)\), the notes write scalar factorization indices \(\kappa_j(x')\), \(b=e^{2\pi i\kappa_j}\), \(\gamma_j=\kappa_j\), and say the class need not contain the system. The qualification is ``operators in ordinary spaces.''''',r'''For a diagonal system the full scalar factorization indices need not lie in a common real strip of width one when one insists on a fixed compatible choice and common Sobolev scale. The eigenvalues of the normal jump determine \(\kappa_j\) only modulo integers; in general \(\gamma_j\equiv\kappa_j\pmod{\mathbb Z}\), not equality for every logarithm branch. Different component orders or variable admissible orders can alter the functional setting, so this observation is a limitation of a specified common-space class, not of elliptic diagonal systems as such.''',r'''The scalar index glyph is read provisionally as \(\kappa\). The equation \(\gamma_j=\kappa_j\) is meaningful only after choosing compatible branches. Pointwise branch adjustment can always narrow a finite set of fractional parts; the possible difficulty is global compatibility with the intended spaces.''',fate=r'''The distinction between \(n_j\), \(\gamma_j\), and \(\kappa_j\) is explicit in \citet[\S1.3, p.~306; \S2.1]{vishikeskin1967normally}; the notebook's example lacks full symbols.''',status=r'''As of 9 November 2026, this is an understood qualification of the formulation. No particular diagonal counterexample can be uniquely recovered.''',refs=['vishikeskin1967normally'],match='partial',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',confidence='medium')
rid=item(127,'P000204','question','Global completion of the boundary Fredholm family',r'''The final line asks when there globally exist \(G\) and an overwritten expression involving \(E\), ``(and \(B\)).'' The connective between \(G\) and \(E\) is not legible enough to decide among the transcription's alternatives. The preceding normal system fixes the mathematical context.''',r'''In the admissible Vishik--Eskin convolution calculus on a smooth bounded domain with compact boundary \(\Gamma\), let \(A_\gamma\), \(\gamma\in S^*\Gamma\), be the normalized continuous Fredholm family, of constant numerical index \(N\). When do globally defined finite matrices of potential and boundary symbols give an invertible normal completion using trivial auxiliary bundles? The precise criterion is \[\operatorname{Ind}(A) = N[1]\quad\hbox{in }K^0(S^*\Gamma).\] Under the paper's regularity and noncritical-order hypotheses this is necessary and sufficient, and the completion can be chosen with \(E=0\). For a connected parameter space, this says that the reduced family-index class vanishes.''',r'''This identifies the definite completion question from the system on P000202; it does not resolve the overwritten connective. Trivial auxiliary bundles, admissible symbol orders, and constant numerical index are essential. Allowing arbitrary auxiliary bundles changes the obstruction. Necessity for a block with \(E\ne0\) also follows from its family index; sufficiency follows from the stronger \(E=0\) construction.''',cls='B',fate=r'''A direct contextual match is \citet[\S3, Theorems~1.3 and 2.3, pp.~328--331]{vishikeskin1967normally}, received 27 April 1967, after this lecture.''',status=r'''As of 9 November 2026, this precise global completion question was solved in 1967. The printed theorem matches the augmented system, jumping indices, global symbols, and obstruction; the source's ambiguous connective prevents a claim of verbatim equivalence.''',refs=['vishikeskin1967normally'],match='partial',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',problem='GP-1966-62',confidence='high')
P.append(dict(id='GP-1966-62',title='Global boundary completion with trivial auxiliary bundles',statement=r'''For the admissible continuous normal Fredholm family of an elliptic convolution system over \(S^*\Gamma\), when can finitely many globally defined potential terms and boundary equations, with trivial auxiliary bundles, produce an invertible completed normal family?''',occurrences=[rid],status=r'''Solved in 1967 under the Vishik--Eskin regularity and noncritical-order hypotheses: the family index must be the class of a signed trivial bundle, and this condition is sufficient with \(E=0\); see \citet[\S3, Theorems~1.3 and 2.3]{vishikeskin1967normally}. Received 27 April 1967; earlier circulation beyond the present seminar evidence was not established. Status checked 9 November 2026. The overwritten connective in the notebook remains uncertain.''',status_date='2026-11',refs=['vishikeskin1967normally']))
correction('P000202-P000204','record date, speaker, and source order','Catalogue record 127: Dynin, 27 March; record 128: Parshin, 20 March','Retain catalogue record order 127 before 128, but record that the handwritten 20 March Parshin heading physically precedes the 27 March factorization lecture; Eskin is separately named on P000203.','The dates 20/III and 27/III are clearly visible on P000202. Neither the March 27 heading nor the Eskin subheading establishes that the catalogue speaker is mistaken.','both','provenance')
correction('P000203','normal-limit symbol for nonzero order','a_-=A(x prime,0,0,-1)','Either first normalize A to order zero, or include the order phase in the negative normal limit according to the chosen convention.','The scan omits normalization; Vishik–Eskin 1967 equation (1.2) has exp(i pi(alpha+i beta)) multiplying the negative limit.','both','ambiguity')
correction('P000203','description of b','measure of nonellipticity','Normal jump or factorization datum of an already elliptic interior symbol.','The scan first calls A elliptic and then uses the phrase nonellipticity. The invertible normal limits do not measure interior nonellipticity.','both','faithful_error')
correction('P000204','gamma versus scalar partial index','gamma_j=kappa_j','Equality only with a specified compatible logarithm branch; generally gamma_j is congruent to kappa_j modulo integers.','The visible exponential b=exp(2 pi i kappa_j) fixes the index only modulo integers. Vishik–Eskin distinguish kappa_j=n_j+gamma_j.','both','ambiguity')
correction('P000204','main global question','G in E / G not in E / G and E','Retain the connective as uncertain. Reconstruct a global potential/boundary completion question from the preceding system, without claiming a diplomatic resolution.','Full-page scan inspection does not distinguish the overwritten connective. The matching Vishik–Eskin theorem is contextual evidence, not a reading of this glyph.','both','ambiguity')
save()

