# Record 129: S. I. Gelfand's report of Bourbaki 319.
talk(129,'1967-03-27','S. I. Gelfand','P000204-P000207','Petrovsky lacunas and Atiyah’s algebraic-geometric formulation','talk',r'''S. Gelfand explicitly reports Atiyah's Bourbaki expos\'e 319, delivered in February 1967 and printed in 1968. The notes define strict hyperbolicity, the retarded fundamental solution and stable lacunas, construct the two Petrovsky homology classes, state the even-order/even-dimension criteria, and begin the residue-integral proof. The shift from \(\mathbb R^n\) to coordinates \(x_0,\ldots,x_n\) is resolved by writing \(N=n+1\). Empty real and complex sections, projective orientation choices, and twisted canonical forms must be distinguished. The notes stop at the Herglotz--Petrovsky formula; later algebraic de Rham proof details are not attributed to the seminar.''')
item(129,'P000204','definition','Strict hyperbolicity of the homogeneous symbol',r'''For a real homogeneous polynomial \(a(\xi_0,\ldots,\xi_n)\) of degree \(m\), every nonzero spatial tuple is required to give exactly \(m\) distinct real roots in \(\xi_0\). The ambient space is first called \(\mathbb R^n\), although \(n+1\) coordinates are then used.''',r'''Write \(N=n+1\). A real homogeneous polynomial \(a\) of degree \(m\) on \(\mathbb R^N\) is strictly hyperbolic with respect to \(e_0\) if \(a(e_0)\ne0\) and, for every \(\xi'\ne0\), the polynomial \(t\mapsto a(t,\xi')\) has \(m\) distinct real roots. The corresponding constant-coefficient differential operator is \(P=a(\partial_x)\), with any Fourier convention declared consistently.''',r'''Noncharacteristic time direction and the distinction between strict hyperbolicity and the weaker real-root condition are supplied. The dimension is corrected uniformly, not by changing subsequent homology degrees.''',fate=r'''Already in \citet[\S1, p.~88]{atiyah1967hyperbolic}, lecture February 1967, print 1968.''',refs=['atiyah1967hyperbolic'],match='exact',literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(129,'P000204','theorem','The retarded fundamental solution',r'''The system \(PE=\delta\), \(E=0\) for \(x_0<0\), has a unique solution by uniqueness for the Cauchy problem.''',r'''For a real homogeneous strictly hyperbolic operator with noncharacteristic time direction, there is a unique distribution \(E\in\mathcal D'(\mathbb R^N)\) satisfying \(PE=\delta_0\) and \(\operatorname{supp}E\subset\{x_0\ge0\}\). More precisely its support lies in the closed forward propagation cone dual to the chosen hyperbolicity cone. Uniqueness uses the vanishing of a homogeneous solution with past-zero support.''',r'''Existence, implicit in ``we seek'' and then ``it is denoted \(E\),'' is distinguished from the stated uniqueness argument. The propagation cone is the convex support cone, a different notion from the projective dual wavefront hypersurface below.''',fate=r'''Classical before the report; \citet[\S1, p.~88]{atiyah1967hyperbolic}. A Fourier--Laplace construction is in \citet[\S3]{atiyahbottgarding1970lacunas}.''',refs=['atiyah1967hyperbolic','atiyahbottgarding1970lacunas'],match='exact')
item(129,'P000204-P000205','theorem','Analyticity away from the dual characteristic hypersurface',r'''The characteristic cone \(A=\{a(\xi)=0\}\) has a dual cone \(B=\{b(x)=0\}\), described by tangent hyperplanes. The solution is analytic in every connected component of its complement.''',r'''For the generic smooth projective characteristic hypersurface \(A\subset\mathbb{CP}^{N-1}\), let \(B\) be the real cone over its projective dual discriminant in the dual projective space. For real \(x\notin B\), the section \(A_x=A\cap\{x\cdot\xi=0\}\) is transverse. The retarded fundamental solution restricts to a real-analytic function on each connected component of \(\{x_0>0\}\setminus B\). The characteristic cone in covector space and its dual in physical space need not have the same defining polynomial.''',r'''``Dual'' here concerns tangent hyperplanes, not the convex polar cone. The generic complex smoothness is supplied as in the next page; this avoids treating every real tangent-locus description as an exact global algebraic equation without qualification.''',fate=r'''The precise geometric setup and analytic statement are in \citet[\S1, pp.~89--91]{atiyah1967hyperbolic}.''',refs=['atiyah1967hyperbolic'],match='exact',literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(129,'P000205','definition','Lacunas and stability under perturbation',r'''A connected component \(C\) is a lacuna if \(E\equiv0\) there; it is stable if this persists for nearby equations.''',r'''A component \(C\) of the complement of the wavefront/dual discriminant in the future half-space is a lacuna in the notebook's sense if \(E_a|_C=0\). It is stable if, for a fixed \(x\in C\), all sufficiently small real homogeneous degree-\(m\) perturbations of \(a\) have fundamental solutions vanishing in a neighborhood of \(x\); the definition is independent of the chosen point in \(C\). The comparison is local or on compact subsets of the moving component, not a requirement that the original entire component remain fixed.''',r'''The perturbation class and meaning of persistence are supplied. Atiyah--Bott--G\aa rding later use a broader notion of lacuna allowing a regular continuation and call the zero-solution case a strong lacuna; their terminology must not silently replace the notebook's.''',fate=r'''Defined by Petrovsky before 1945 \citep[pp.~290--291]{petrovsky1945lacunas}; the formulation follows \citet[pp.~89--90]{atiyah1967hyperbolic}.''',status=r'''As of 9 November 2026, the definition and its homological criteria are classical. Later terminology is distinguished in \citet[\S9--10]{atiyahbottgarding1970lacunas}.''',refs=['petrovsky1945lacunas','atiyah1967hyperbolic','atiyahbottgarding1970lacunas'],match='exact',literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(129,'P000205','method','Reduction to a nonsingular complex characteristic hypersurface',r'''The real polynomial defines \(A\subset\mathbb{CP}^n\), assumed to have no complex singularities. The notes say a small real change of coefficients can achieve this.''',r'''Strict hyperbolicity is open in the space of real homogeneous degree-\(m\) polynomials. The complex singular-hypersurface discriminant is a proper algebraic subset of that coefficient space. Consequently a strictly hyperbolic polynomial can be approximated by real strictly hyperbolic polynomials with nonsingular complex projective zero locus. This generic reduction is legitimate for a question defined through stability under all sufficiently small admissible perturbations.''',r'''Strict hyperbolicity alone already gives smoothness of the real projective zero locus, but not complex smoothness. The reduction does not prove that the original complex variety was nonsingular.''',fate=r'''The prelecture reduction is \citet[\S1, p.~90]{atiyah1967hyperbolic}.''',refs=['atiyah1967hyperbolic'],match='exact',literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(129,'P000205','construction','The real hyperplane-section cycle beta',r'''The notes form \(A_x=A\cap\{x\cdot\xi=0\}\), call \((A_x)_{\mathbb R}\) a cycle of dimension \(n-2\), and write \(\beta_x\in H_{n-2}(A_x)\). The phrase ``a sum of spheres'' is attached to this section.''',r'''Assume \(N>2\) and \(m,N\) even, with \(A\subset\mathbb{CP}^{N-1}\) smooth and \(x\notin B\). Hyperbolicity gives \(m/2\) spherical components of \(A_{\mathbb R}\), each of real dimension \(N-2\), with compatible orientations from the hyperbolicity direction. A trivialization of the real hyperplane line bundle over \(A_{\mathbb R}\) makes \(x\cdot\xi\) a real function. Its transverse zero locus is oriented and defines \[\beta_x=[(A_x)_{\mathbb R}]\in H_{N-3}(A_x;\mathbb C).\]''',r'''The coefficient field and orientation data are restored from the identified source. Atiyah's sphere decomposition concerns \(A_{\mathbb R}\), not a stated decomposition of every \((A_x)_{\mathbb R}\); the notebook's placement of that phrase is not accepted as a general theorem about hyperplane sections.''',fate=r'''Exact cycle construction: \citet[\S1, p.~91]{atiyah1967hyperbolic}; the sphere phrase has only a partial match.''',refs=['atiyah1967hyperbolic'],match='partial',literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(129,'P000205','construction','The wave operator: an empty real section, not an empty complex quadric',r'''For \(a=\xi_0^2-\sum_{j=1}^n\xi_j^2\) and \(x=(1,0,\ldots,0)\), the notes obtain \(\xi_0=0\), \(\sum\xi_j^2=0\), then say that in projective space this has no points.''',r'''The real projective section is empty because a sum of real squares vanishes only at the zero vector; hence \(\beta_x=0\). The complex projective section is nonempty for \(n\ge2\): for example \([\xi_0:\xi_1:\xi_2:\cdots]=[0:1:i:0:\cdots]\) lies on it. If \(N=n+1>2\) is even, the order \(m=2<N\) and the Petrovsky criterion yields the familiar stable interior lacuna of the retarded wave solution.''',r'''\ours\ The explicit complex point checks the literal error independently of the real positive-definiteness argument. The parity and dimension restriction is essential: the empty real section by itself does not assert Huygens' principle in every dimension.''',fate=r'''The wave example is classical \citep[p.~289]{petrovsky1945lacunas}; see \citet[pp.~88--89, Theorem~1]{atiyah1967hyperbolic}.''',status=r'''As of 9 November 2026, the corrected example is standard; the literal claim of no complex points is false for the dimensions used in the theorem.''',refs=['petrovsky1945lacunas','atiyah1967hyperbolic'],match='partial',literal='false as written',development='annotated')
item(129,'P000206','construction','The signed relative cycle alpha and its boundary',r'''The signed chain \(\{x\cdot\xi>0\}-\{x\cdot\xi<0\}\) on \(A_{\mathbb R}\) defines \(\alpha_x\in H_{n-1}(A,A_x)\), with \(\partial\alpha_x=2\beta_x\).''',r'''In the same oriented even-parity setting, let \(f_x\) denote the real function induced by the linear form after trivializing the real hyperplane line bundle over \(A_{\mathbb R}\). The signed fundamental chain \(\operatorname{sgn}(f_x)[A_{\mathbb R}]\) is a relative cycle of \((A,A_x)\), defining \(\alpha_x\in H_{N-2}(A,A_x;\mathbb C)\). With consistent boundary orientation, \(\partial\alpha_x=2\beta_x\), because the two sides contribute with the same boundary sign after the negative side is subtracted.''',r'''The sign of a homogeneous linear form is not intrinsically defined on a real projective point without a lift/trivialization. The source supplies exactly this missing choice. \(\alpha\) is relative, not generally an absolute cycle.''',fate=r'''The construction and factor two appear in \citet[\S1, pp.~91--92]{atiyah1967hyperbolic}.''',refs=['atiyah1967hyperbolic'],match='exact',literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(129,'P000206','theorem','Constancy of Petrovsky classes within a chamber',r'''When \(x\) moves in one connected component of the cone complement, \(\alpha_x\) and \(\beta_x\) do not change; the notation becomes \(\alpha_C,\beta_C\).''',r'''Over a component \(C\) of the complement of the dual discriminant, the smooth hyperplane sections and pairs \((A,A_x)\) form locally trivial families. Their homology groups are identified by continuation along paths, under which the geometrically defined classes \(\alpha_x\) and \(\beta_x\) are locally constant. In particular their vanishing is independent of the chosen \(x\in C\). This is a statement in the corresponding homology local systems, not literal equality of elements of different groups without identification.''',r'''The local-system interpretation supplies the missing comparison maps; no global choice of an entire homology basis or absence of monodromy is asserted.''',fate=r'''Stated before the two criteria in \citet[\S1, p.~92]{atiyah1967hyperbolic}.''',refs=['atiyah1967hyperbolic'],match='exact',literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(129,'P000206','theorem','Stable-lacuna criterion below the dimension',r'''If the order \(m\) and the dimension \(n+1\) are even and \(m<n+1\), \(\beta_C=0\) is necessary and sufficient for a stable lacuna.''',r'''Let \(N>2\) and \(m\) be even, \(m<N\), and let \(a\) be real homogeneous and strictly hyperbolic with smooth complex characteristic hypersurface. A component \(C\subset\{x_0>0\}\setminus B\) is a stable lacuna in the zero-solution sense if and only if \(\beta_C=0\) in \(H_{N-3}(A_x;\mathbb C)\).''',r'''Complex homology coefficients, complex smoothness, strict hyperbolicity, and \(N>2\) are supplied from the report's precise source. An integral-homology torsion obstruction is not part of this theorem.''',fate=r'''Petrovsky's theorem, restated in \citet[Theorem~1, p.~92]{atiyah1967hyperbolic}, circulated in the February 1967 lecture before this report.''',status=r'''As of 9 November 2026, the criterion is established. Atiyah's June 1967 addendum additionally says that in this low-order regime every lacuna is stable; the addendum postdates the seminar. Later broader formulations appear in \citet[\S10]{atiyahbottgarding1970lacunas}.''',refs=['atiyah1967hyperbolic','atiyahbottgarding1970lacunas'],match='exact',literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(129,'P000206','theorem','Stable-lacuna criterion at or above the dimension',r'''If \(m\ge n+1\), with \(m,n+1\) even, \(\alpha_C=0\) is necessary and sufficient for a stable lacuna.''',r'''Under the same strict-hyperbolicity and smoothness assumptions, let \(N>2\) and \(m\) be even with \(m\ge N\). Then \(C\) is a stable lacuna if and only if \(\alpha_C=0\) in \(H_{N-2}(A,A_x;\mathbb C)\). The necessary condition \(\beta_C=0\), obtained from \(\partial\alpha_C=2\beta_C\), is weaker and cannot replace the relative-class condition in this regime.''',r'''The parity, homology field, and regularity are retained from the exact source. This statement addresses stability, not the classification of all accidental lacunas or all singular wavefronts.''',fate=r'''The exact statement is \citet[Theorem~2, p.~92]{atiyah1967hyperbolic}, after Petrovsky and before the March report.''',status=r'''As of 9 November 2026, the criterion is classical. Atiyah's June 1967 addendum excludes nontrivial stable lacunas in this even-dimensional high-order regime; later terminology calls the notebook's zero regions strong lacunas \citep[pp.~185--188]{atiyahbottgarding1970lacunas}.''',refs=['atiyah1967hyperbolic','atiyahbottgarding1970lacunas'],match='exact',literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(129,'P000206','construction','The projective canonical bundle and Euler form',r'''The notes write \(K_{\mathbb{CP}^n}\simeq\mathcal O(-n-1)\), assert a unique holomorphic \(n\)-form with one pole of order \(n+1\), and give \(\omega=\sum(-1)^j x_j\,dx_0\wedge\cdots\widehat{dx_j}\cdots\wedge dx_n\).''',r'''With \(N=n+1\), \(K_{\mathbb{CP}^{N-1}}\simeq\mathcal O(-N)\) and \(H^0(K(N))\simeq\mathbb C\), whereas \(H^0(K)=0\). Thus a nonzero generator is unique up to scalar as a twisted canonical section, or as a meromorphic top form with poles of order at most \(N\) along a fixed hyperplane. On the affine cone its homogeneous representative is \[\omega=\sum_{j=0}^{N-1}(-1)^j\xi_j\,d\xi_0\wedge\cdots\wedge\widehat{d\xi_j}\wedge\cdots\wedge d\xi_{N-1}.\]''',r'''The coordinates are renamed \(\xi\) to distinguish symbol space from the physical variable. The cone expression has homogeneity \(N\); it is not an ordinary global holomorphic top form on projective space. A fixed polar hyperplane and uniqueness up to scalar replace the literal ``unique holomorphic form with a pole.''''',fate=r'''The exact twisted-bundle formulation is \citet[\S2, p.~93]{atiyah1967hyperbolic}.''',refs=['atiyah1967hyperbolic'],match='exact',literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(129,'P000207','construction','Poincare residue with the required homogeneity',r'''The residue form is introduced by \(\omega/a=(da/a)\wedge\omega_a+\Theta\). An overwritten parenthesis appears to say that \(\Theta\) is zero on \(a=0\).''',r'''For a smooth hypersurface \(A=\{a=0\}\), the local decomposition of a meromorphic top form with a simple pole is \(\omega/a=(da/a)\wedge\eta+\Theta\), where \(\Theta\) is holomorphic and \(\omega_a=\eta|_A\) is the residue. With homogeneous projective representatives, \(\omega_a\) is a section of \(K_A\otimes\mathcal O(N-m)\); since \(K_A\simeq\mathcal O(m-N)\), the twist is essential unless \(m=N\). Multiplying by a homogeneous polynomial of degree \(m-N\), when \(m\ge N\), produces an ordinary holomorphic top form on \(A\).''',r'''The pullback of the holomorphic remainder to the hypersurface vanishes by degree; its coefficient functions need not vanish there. The exact overwritten parenthesis is not resolved by this interpretation. Local residues glue with the displayed twist, rather than as an untwisted form in every degree.''',fate=r'''The residue and line-bundle twists are explicit in \citet[\S2, p.~93]{atiyah1967hyperbolic}.''',refs=['atiyah1967hyperbolic'],match='exact',literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
item(129,'P000207','theorem','The Herglotz–Petrovsky residue integral',r'''For \(m\ge n+1\) the final formula is \(E(x)=c\int_{A_{\mathbb R}}\operatorname{sgn}(x\cdot\xi)(x\cdot\xi)^{m-n-1}\omega_a\), followed by a reference to the first volume of generalized functions.''',r'''Under the smooth, strictly hyperbolic setting above, with \(m,N\) even and \(m\ge N>2\), the analytic value of the retarded fundamental solution for \(x_0>0\), away from the dual discriminant, is \[E(x)=c_{m,N}\int_{A_{\mathbb R}}\operatorname{sgn}(f_x)\,(x\cdot\xi)^{m-N}\omega_a.\] Orientations and the real line-bundle trivialization are those used for \(\alpha_x\). The polynomial factor cancels the projective twist of the residue; the normalization constant depends on the Fourier convention, the chosen generator, and the normalization of \(P\). The fundamental solution is understood distributionally on the wavefront.''',r'''The parity and dimension restrictions are inherited from the theorem, not repeated in the final notebook line. The shorthand constant is not assigned an unsupported numerical value. The formula supplies the announced proof route, but the omitted converse uses substantial algebraic geometry and is not proved in these pages.''',fate=r'''The exact formula is \citet[\S2, equation (2.1), p.~93]{atiyah1967hyperbolic}; its classical origin is described in \citet[Chapter~III]{petrovsky1945lacunas}.''',status=r'''As of 9 November 2026, this residue representation is established. Generalized formulas and their conventions are developed in \citet[\S7]{atiyahbottgarding1970lacunas}.''',refs=['atiyah1967hyperbolic','petrovsky1945lacunas','atiyahbottgarding1970lacunas'],match='exact',literal='correct with missing hypotheses')
correction('P000204','ambient dimension','Operator in R^n, then coordinates x_0,...,x_n','Use N=n+1 throughout this report; preserve the subsequent n-based projective and homology indices.','The scan explicitly lists n+1 coordinates and the theorem on P000206 calls n+1 the dimension.','both','faithful_error')
correction('P000205','sphere decomposition beside beta','The real hyperplane section is a sum of spheres','The matching source decomposes A_R into m/2 spheres; it does not state this decomposition for every (A_x)_R. Retain the notebook wording with a scope warning.','Atiyah Bourbaki 319 p.91 states the decomposition before introducing the hyperplane section. The scan attaches the phrase to the latter.','both','ambiguity')
correction('P000205','wave equation projective section','The section sum xi_j squared=0 has no projective points','Its real projective locus is empty. Its complex locus contains [1:i:0:...] when there are at least two coordinates.','The scan calls A complex earlier on the same page. The displayed complex point is a direct counterexample to the unrestricted assertion.','both','faithful_error')
correction('P000206','signed chain on projective space','sign(x dot xi) without a choice of representative','Trivialize the real hyperplane line bundle on A_R, or make an equivalent compatible lift and orientation choice.','Changing a homogeneous representative xi to -xi reverses the sign; Atiyah pp.91–92 supplies the missing trivialization.','both','ambiguity')
correction('P000206','homology coefficients and theorem hypotheses','alpha and beta in unspecified homology; m and n+1 even','Use complex coefficients, N=n+1>2, strict hyperbolicity, and a smooth complex characteristic hypersurface.','These conditions are explicit in Atiyah pp.90–92; torsion-sensitive integral homology is not the stated criterion.','both','ambiguity')
correction('P000206','unique holomorphic form with a pole','A unique holomorphic n-form with one pole of order n+1; no global sections hence uniqueness','K_CP^n(n+1) has a one-dimensional space of sections. Equivalently fix a polar hyperplane; uniqueness is up to a scalar.','Atiyah p.93 states the twisted section-space assertion. Vanishing of H^0(K) alone does not establish the notebook assertion.','both','faithful_error')
correction('P000207','residue remainder and projective twisting','Theta=0 on a=0; omega_a used as an ordinary form','Theta is a holomorphic remainder with zero pullback by degree, not necessarily vanishing coefficients; retain the overwritten last glyph as uncertain. The residue carries twist O(N-m).','Scan inspection leaves the overwritten parenthesis uncertain. The local residue identity and Atiyah p.93 determine the valid invariant formulation.','both','ambiguity')
